## 4. Read the logs — the definitive step

When the checks above are inconclusive, turn up debug and read the actual LDAP query
SSSD issued. This almost always makes the cause obvious in a single line.

```ini
# /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
[domain/corp.example.com]
debug_level = 9

[nss]
debug_level = 9
```

```bash
systemctl restart sssd
sss_cache -E
id alice                       # reproduce the failure

grep -iE 'base|filter|search' /var/log/sssd/sssd_corp.example.com.log | tail -40
```

Look for the search base and LDAP filter SSSD used, and whether the reply contained
zero entries or entries missing attributes. Turn `debug_level` back down afterwards —
level 9 is extremely verbose.

Useful log files:

| File | Contains |
|---|---|
| `sssd_<domain>.log` | LDAP/Kerberos backend, search bases, access decisions |
| `sssd_nss.log` | `getent` / `id` resolution path |
| `sssd_pam.log` | Login-time authentication and account checks |
| `krb5_child.log` | Kerberos ticket acquisition failures |
| `ldap_child.log` | GSSAPI bind failures against LDAP |

