## 5. If identity works but login is denied

Different problem, different suspects. Symptom in `journalctl -u sshd`:

```
Access denied for user alice by PAM account configuration
```

Diagnostic:

```bash
sssctl user-checks alice -s sshd -a acct
```

### 5.1 AD GPO enforcement — the most common cause

RHEL 8+ defaults to `ad_gpo_access_control = enforcing`. SSSD independently evaluates
the AD Group Policy rights **"Allow log on locally"** and **"Allow log on through
Remote Desktop Services"**. Users who pass every LDAP check are still denied if those
GPO rights don't list them.

Test without changing behaviour:

```ini
[domain/corp.example.com]
ad_gpo_access_control = permissive
```

Permissive mode logs what it *would* have denied. If the denials disappear, the GPO is
the cause — fix it in AD rather than leaving enforcement off.

```bash
grep -i gpo /var/log/sssd/sssd_corp.example.com.log | tail -30
```

### 5.2 `simple_allow_groups` name mismatch

The value must match the **resolved** group name. With
`use_fully_qualified_names = True`, write `unixadmins@corp.example.com`, not
`unixadmins`.

### 5.3 Membership only via primary GID

If the allow-group is the user's primary group, confirm the group object itself
resolves and lists the user:

```bash
getent group unixadmins
id alice
```

### 5.4 `ldap_access_order` / `ldap_access_filter`

```bash
grep -E 'ldap_access_order|ldap_access_filter|access_provider' /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
```

An `expire` order or a custom filter on `accountExpires` / `userAccountControl` can
deny accounts that otherwise look healthy.

