## 6. Backend offline / Kerberos failures

If `sssctl domain-status` reports **Offline**, identity lookups serve stale cache only —
accounts created after the host went offline will appear not to exist. Work through:

```bash
# 1. Time skew — breaks Kerberos above ~5 minutes
timedatectl
chronyc sources -v

# 2. Reachability and DNS
ping -c2 dc01.corp.example.com
dig +short _ldap._tcp.corp.example.com SRV
cat /etc/resolv.conf

# 3. Host keytab validity
klist -kte /etc/krb5.keytab
kinit -k && klist

# 4. What SSSD says
grep -iE 'offline|error|denied|skew|cannot' /var/log/sssd/sssd_corp.example.com.log | tail -40
```

### Keytab gotchas

- **`ipa-getkeytab` / `adcli` append, they don't replace.** Multiple KVNOs accumulate
  in one file and Kerberos selects the **highest** KVNO — which may be a stale key.
  When re-keying, delete the keytab first rather than fetching on top of it.
- **`hostname -f` must match the enrolled principal.** A short hostname produces
  `host/shortname@` with an empty realm and fails preauth immediately.
- **A cloned VM inherits the source host's identity.** If `/etc/krb5.keytab`,
  `/etc/ipa/default.conf`, and `/etc/sssd/sssd.conf` all reference a different machine's
  name, the clone is impersonating it — and re-keying breaks the original host.
- **Forward and reverse DNS must agree** with the enrolled name. Kerberos canonicalises
  via DNS; a mismatch causes intermittent, hard-to-read failures.
- **Pin the name on enrollment** with `--hostname=`. Uninstall routines can restore a
  stale hostname from `/var/lib/ipa-client/sysrestore/`, and the subsequent install will
  silently enroll under the wrong name.

### Cache reset

`sss_cache -E` is often not enough. Full reset:

```bash
systemctl stop sssd
rm -rf /var/lib/sss/db/*
systemctl start sssd
```

